      ":question/problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/generate/scratch/17592186235285/assets/ac1fe506b07f26dfcb03456b-prompt.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. Given that $c=\\lambda a+b,$ find $\\lambda .$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "To find $\\lambda ,$ we want to match the expression\n\n$$\nc=\\lambda a+b.\n$$\n\nWe are given that one of the components is $b.$ So, we first draw the vector $b$ from the origin. Then we complete the path to the point $C.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/generate/scratch/17592186235285/assets/21ddf5fb6f2cda8c4d3edfdd-solution.png)\n\nLet $P$ be the endpoint of the vector $b.$ Then\n\n$$\nc=\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PC}.\n$$\n\nIn $\u25b3OPC,$ we have:\n\n- $PC=4|a|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{PC}$ and $a$ have opposite directions\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{PC}=-4a.$\n\nAdditionally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c & =\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PC} \\\\  & =b-4a.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $c=\\lambda a+b=-4a+b.$\n\nSince $a\u2226b,$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients of $a,$ we get:\n\n$$\n\\lambda =-4\n$$",
